            <p><strong>Additional savings on accommodation and travel:</strong> Only one flight, one hotel stay, one rehabilitation period — the total treatment journey costs roughly 60% of doing each knee separately.</p>

            <!-- SECTION 5: Patient Journey (EXPANDED) -->
            <h2>Section 5: Patient Journey — From First Consultation to Full Recovery</h2>

            <p>Most international patients complete their knee replacement journey in 18-24 days. Here's a day-by-day breakdown of what to expect:</p>

            <h3>Phase 1: Pre-Travel (Weeks 2-4 Before Departure)</h3>
            <div class="highlight-box">
                <p><strong>Day -21 to -14:</strong> Send your medical records, X-rays, and MRI scans to your chosen hospital for review. Include any relevant blood work and a letter from your current physician.</p>

                <p><strong>Day -14 to -10:</strong> Receive your personalized treatment plan, cost estimate, and surgeon bio. Schedule a video consultation with your assigned international coordinator.</p>

                <p><strong>Day -10 to -7:</strong> Apply for medical visa (M visa). The hospital provides an official invitation letter. Book flights and arrange airport transfer with the hospital's patient services.</p>

                <p><strong>Day -7 to -1:</strong> Prepare questions for your surgeon, arrange post-return care with your local doctor, and purchase travel insurance that covers medical complications abroad.</p>
            </div>

            <h3>Phase 2: Arrival and Pre-Op (Days 1-2)</h3>
            <ol>
                <li><strong>Day 1:</strong> Land at Beijing/Shanghai/Guangzhou airport. Hospital driver meets you with a name sign. Transfer to partner hotel or hospital guest room.</li>
                <li><strong>Day 1 evening:</strong> Rest and adjust to time zone. Light meals recommended.</li>
                <li><strong>Day 2 morning:</strong> Comprehensive pre-operative assessment at the hospital:
                    <ul>
                        <li>Blood tests (CBC, coagulation, infectious disease screening)</li>
                        <li>ECG and chest X-ray</li>
                        <li>Final knee imaging and surgical planning</li>
                    </ul>
                </li>
                <li><strong>Day 2 afternoon:</strong> Meet your surgeon for final consultation. Review implant options (standard vs. high-flexibility). Sign consent forms.</li>
            </ol>

            <h3>Phase 3: Surgery and Hospital Stay (Days 3-9)</h3>
            <div class="highlight-box">
                <p><strong>Day 3 (Surgery Day):</strong></p>
                <ul>
                    <li>Nothing by mouth after midnight</li>
                    <li>Pre-operative medications (antibiotic prophylaxis)</li>
                    <li>Surgery duration: 1-2 hours for single knee, 2.5-4 hours for bilateral</li>
                    <li>Recovery room observation: 2-4 hours</li>
                    <li>Transfer to private/semi-private room</li>
                </ul>

                <p><strong>Days 4-5 (Early Recovery):</strong></p>
                <ul>
                    <li>Pain management with IV medications transitioning to oral</li>
                    <li>Physical therapist visits twice daily</li>
                    <li>First assisted standing: Day 4</li>
                    <li>First steps with walker: Day 4-5</li>
                    <li> wound drainage removal</li>
                </ul>

                <p><strong>Days 6-7 (Mobility Progress):</strong></p>
                <ul>
                    <li>Transition from walker to cane</li>
                    <li>Climbing stairs with assistance</li>
                    <li>Wound inspection and dressing change</li>
                    <li>Discharge planning with rehab instructions</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <h3>Phase 4: Outpatient Recovery (Days 10-18)</h3>
            <p>After hospital discharge, most patients stay at a nearby hotel and return to the hospital daily for outpatient physiotherapy:</p>
            <ol>
                <li><strong>Days 10-12:</strong> Intensive physiotherapy sessions (2 hours daily). Focus on range of motion exercises and gentle strengthening.</li>
                <li><strong>Days 13-14:</strong> Continued rehab. Stitch/suture removal typically on Day 12-14.</li>
                <li><strong>Days 15-18:</strong> Advanced walking exercises, balance training, and discharge readiness assessment.</li>
            </ol>

            <h3>Phase 5: Final Clearance and Return (Days 18-24)</h3>
            <div class="highlight-box">
                <p><strong>Day 18-20:</strong> Final orthopedic evaluation. X-rays confirm implant positioning. Surgeon provides written clearance to fly.</p>

                <p><strong>Day 20-22:</strong> Travel home. Most patients fly economy class with aisle seats for easier movement. Compression stockings and mobility aids recommended.</p>

                <p><strong>Week 4-6 home:</strong> Continue prescribed home exercises. Follow-up with local orthopedic surgeon per the discharge records provided.</p>
            </div>

            <h3>Recovery Timeline Expectations</h3>
            <table class="cost-table">
                <thead>
                    <tr>
                        <th>Milestone</th>
                        <th>Typical Timeline</th>
                    </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr>
                        <td>Walking with walker</td>
                        <td>Day 4-5</td>
